<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section>
  <title>Project Rubric</title>

  <p>
    The Numerical Data Project is worth <alert>30 points</alert>. This
    rubric expands the general project rubric to describe the expectations
    for this report.
  </p>
  <p>
    Complete a one-mean <m>t</m> confidence interval or hypothesis test,
    a paired <m>t</m> or independent-samples Welch <m>t</m> hypothesis
    test, and a one-way ANOVA with three or more independent groups.
    Include Tukey comparisons when the ANOVA is statistically significant.
    Either option in each choice receives the same available credit.
    Credit depends on the quality of the work rather than whether a
    hypothesis test is statistically significant.
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Data Set Selection and Documentation: 2 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The data contain appropriate
        numerical responses and the group or paired structure needed
        for the selected one-mean analysis, two-mean test, and one-way
        ANOVA with at least three independent groups.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The source of each data set and
        relevant documentation are identified. Data preparation and
        important modifications are documented.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Data Set Summary and Research Questions: 4 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The context, observational units,
        population of interest, and usable sample sizes for the analyses
        are clearly explained.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The numerical responses and units,
        group labels, and any pairing or difference order are clearly
        identified.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Data collection and relevant data
        preparation are described when that information is available.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Three meaningful research questions
        match the selected one-mean analysis, two-mean hypothesis test,
        and one-way ANOVA. The entire section is no longer than one page.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Graphical Analysis: 5 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Numerical summary tables correctly
        report sample sizes, means, and standard deviations for the
        relevant samples or groups and, for the paired option, for the
        differences.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> An appropriate histogram of the
        one-mean sample is constructed and used to describe its distribution.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Side-by-side boxplots correctly
        compare all ANOVA groups, with attention to centers, spreads,
        and unusual observations.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The selected two-mean test is
        supported by an appropriate plot of paired differences or a
        comparison of the two independent group distributions. Graphs
        may be reused when the relevant groups are clearly identified.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Tables and graphs are readable,
        use suitable titles, labels, units, scales, and legends, and
        are discussed in relation to the questions and procedure conditions.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Statistical Methods and Computations: 5 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The selected one-mean <m>t</m>
        confidence interval or hypothesis test is appropriate and
        performed correctly with the relevant sample statistics.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The paired <m>t</m> or independent
        Welch <m>t</m> hypothesis test matches the study design and is
        performed correctly, including the pairing or group order and
        relevant sample statistics.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The one-way ANOVA is performed
        correctly for at least three independent groups. The ANOVA
        table and relevant calculations are reported, and Tukey
        comparisons are performed correctly when the ANOVA is <alert>significant</alert>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The conditions for the selected
        procedures are assessed using the study design and relevant
        numerical and graphical evidence, including paired differences
        or within-group distributions as appropriate.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Population parameters, hypotheses
        for tests, confidence or significance levels, statistical notation,
        degrees of freedom, and relevant R commands are handled appropriately.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Results and Interpretation: 5 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The one-mean confidence interval
        or hypothesis-test result is reported accurately and interpreted
        in terms of the population mean and the original research question.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The paired or independent two-mean
        test result is reported accurately and interpreted in context,
        with the correct difference direction and a conclusion supported
        by the <m>p</m>-value or critical value.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The ANOVA result is reported
        accurately and interpreted as evidence about whether all
        population means are equal, without claiming that a significant
        ANOVA identifies the differing pairs.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> When the ANOVA is significant,
        Tukey adjusted <m>p</m>-values are interpreted correctly and
        significant pairs are described with direction and observed
        differences. When the ANOVA is not significant, the report
        accurately explains the result and why follow-up comparisons
        are not pursued for this project.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The discussion connects numerical
        findings to the research questions and their practical meaning,
        and keeps generalizations and causal claims consistent with
        the study design.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Conclusion: 5 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>2 points:</alert> The conclusion answers all three
        research questions and accurately summarizes the principal
        findings, including Tukey findings when applicable.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The findings are explained in
        the scientific context, including the practical meaning of
        observed means or differences when relevant.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Important limitations of the data,
        sampling, study design, or procedure conditions are discussed.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The conclusion is a clear,
        coherent discussion and is no longer than one page.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Organization and Presentation: 4 points</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The report follows a logical
        sequence from the research questions through the analyses
        to the conclusions.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The report uses complete
        sentences and clear, professional language.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> Tables, graphs, computations,
        and statistical results are placed logically and referenced
        in the surrounding text.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>1 point:</alert> The report uses readable,
        consistent formatting, appropriate rounding, and selected
        software output with explanations.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <table xml:id="tab-num-project-rubric-summary">
    <title>Project Point Summary</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="left" valign="top"
             top="medium" left="medium" bottom="medium" right="medium">
      <col width="82%"/>
      <col width="18%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Criterion</cell><cell>Points</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>Data Set Selection and Documentation</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Data Set Summary and Research Questions</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Graphical Analysis</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Statistical Methods and Computations</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Results and Interpretation</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Conclusion</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
      <row><cell>Organization and Presentation</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
      <row top="medium"><cell><alert>Total</alert></cell><cell><alert>30</alert></cell></row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
</section>
